The requirement parser was never reachable, and it is in this image all along.

NO REQUIREMENT STRING IN THIS SYSTEM COULD BE PARSED. Every designated requirement sealing a piece
of nested code was refused, and so was the fixed Apple-authored string inside isAppleDeveloperCert.
That is one defect appearing twice, not two coincidences, and it presented as
errSecCSResourcesInvalid, "the sealed resource directory is invalid", which points at the
application plist. It is not the plist: the seal parses perfectly, 499 files and 13 rules.

Apple keeps the requirement grammar out of the main Security binary and in a loadable bundle,
csparser.bundle, so the ANTLR runtime is only paged in when something compiles a requirement. FOUR
things stood between us and it, each hiding the next:

  1. Security.framework has NO Info.plist, so CFBundleGetBundleWithIdentifier for com.apple.security
     returns NULL and the plugin path is never computed.
  2. No PlugIns symlink at the framework root, so the computed path does not resolve.
  3. csparser.bundle is never INSTALLED into the framework, though it is built.
  4. And the bundle is 4112 BYTES OF NOTHING: csparser.cpp is 23 lines whose entire body is
     asm(".reference _findAntlrPlugin"), a stub that REFERENCES the symbol. The buck target links the
     stub and none of the ANTLR sources that define it.

THIS BUILD DOES NOT SEPARATE THEM. antlrplugin.cpp and the whole antlr2 runtime are compiled into
security_codesigning_obj, which is linked into Security itself, so findAntlrPlugin is right here --
merely absent from the export list, which is exactly why looking it up through a bundle could never
work. So ask for it directly and keep the bundle route below for a build that does separate them.

MEASURED, and the verdict moved again:

    -67055  errSecCSResourcesInvalid   nothing could be parsed at all
    -67021  errSecCSBadNestedCode      two named components fail their designated requirement:
              /Applications/MoneyMoney.app/Contents/MacOS/UpdateHelper
              /Applications/MoneyMoney.app/Contents/XPCServices/com.moneymoney-app.update.xpc

isAppleDeveloperCert now RETURNS a value instead of throwing, and returns false. The failure has
moved from PARSING a requirement to EVALUATING one, which is a different piece of work: the
evaluator has to match anchor apple generic and the certificate field clauses against the chain.

MoneyMoney still shows its damaged-file alert. Looked at, in the comparable no-launchd
configuration, and said plainly: -67021 is still a failure.

The traces added here are what made this findable, all under CIDER_TRACE_SECITEM: the plugin host
says whether the parser was found in-image, whether the framework bundle resolved, where the bundle
loads from, whether the load succeeded and whether the ENTRY POINT resolved -- a separate failure
from the load, and the first version of that trace sat before the lookup and so printed loaded while
the lookup was the thing failing. The parser's own error text is printed rather than thrown into a
CFError nobody reads. validateNestedCode prints the requirement it was handed. ResourceSeal names
which of its four identical refusals fired, and the collecting context names every resource problem
as it is gathered rather than only the one status thrown at the end -- which is how the two named
components above became visible.
